    "id": "mixture_of_experts__moe_load_balancing",
    "domain": "mixture_of_experts",
    "domainLabel": "Mixture Of Experts",
    "algo": "moe_load_balancing",
    "algoName": "Moe Load Balancing",
    "title": "Rigorous Mathematical Specification, Auxiliary Entropy Loss, and Buffer Capacity of MoE Load Balancing",
    "excerpt": "Unconstrained top-k routing suffers from routing collapse: the router over-allocates tokens to a small subset of experts, causing hardware execution starvation on idle experts and buffer overflow on p...",
    "tex": "../mixture_of_experts/moe_load_balancing/math.tex",
    "pdf": "../mixture_of_experts/moe_load_balancing/math.pdf",
    "lines": 128,
    "rawTex": "\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n\\usepackage{algorithm}\n\\usepackage{algpseudocode}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\theoremstyle{definition}\n\\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}[section]\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}[section]\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Rigorous Mathematical Specification, Auxiliary Entropy Loss, and Buffer Capacity of MoE Load Balancing}}\n\\author{Team Scaibu \\\\ \\small Email: \\texttt{jaydeep.9664920749@gmail.com} \\\\ \\small Architecture and Core Engine Team}\n\\date{October 2026}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nUnconstrained top-$k$ routing suffers from routing collapse: the router over-allocates tokens to a small subset of experts, causing hardware execution starvation on idle experts and buffer overflow on popular experts. This specification formalizes the auxiliary load balancing loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = E \\sum_{e} f_e P_e$, proves that minimal loss occurs under uniform distribution, defines buffer capacity limits, and verifies step-by-step numerical execution.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Notation and Mathematical Preliminaries}\n\nLet $T \\in \\mathbb{N}_{\\ge 1}$ denote the number of tokens in a batch, and let $E \\in \\mathbb{N}_{\\ge 1}$ denote the number of experts.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $P_{t, e} \\in [0, 1]$: Router softmax probability for token $t$ and expert $e$.\n    \\item $P_e = \\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=1}^T P_{t, e}$: Average router probability assigned to expert $e$.\n    \\item $f_e = \\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=1}^T \\mathbb{I}(\\operatorname{argmax} \\mathbf{P}_t = e)$: Fraction of tokens routed to expert $e$.\n    \\item $C_{\\text{factor}} \\ge 1.0$: Capacity factor multiplier.\n    \\item $C_{\\text{expert}} = \\left\\lceil \\frac{T}{E} \\cdot C_{\\text{factor}} \\right\\rceil$: Maximum buffer capacity per expert.\n    \\item $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = E \\sum_{e=1}^E f_e P_e$: Auxiliary load balancing loss.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Mathematical Notation for MoE Load Balancing}\n\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Symbol} & \\textbf{Domain} & \\textbf{Description} \\\\\n\\midrule\n$T, E$ & $\\mathbb{N}_{\\ge 1}$ & Batch tokens and expert counts \\\\\n$C_{\\text{factor}}$ & $\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 1.0}$ & Buffer capacity scaling multiplier \\\\\n$C_{\\text{expert}}$ & $\\mathbb{N}_{\\ge 1}$ & Maximum token buffer per expert \\\\\n$f_e$ & $[0, 1]$ & Empirical dispatch fraction for expert $e$ \\\\\n$P_e$ & $[0, 1]$ & Mean routing probability for expert $e$ \\\\\n$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}}$ & $\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 1.0}$ & Load balancing penalty \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Problem Definition and Algorithmic Execution}\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[H]\n\\caption{MoE Load Balancing Loss and Capacity Allocation}\n\\label{alg:moe_load_balance}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\Require Router probabilities $\\mathbf{P} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{T \\times E}$, capacity factor $C_{\\text{factor}} \\ge 1.0$, experts $E$\n\\Ensure Aux loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}}$, expert counts $\\mathbf{c}$, dropped count $D$, drop rate $r_{\\text{drop}}$\n\\State $T \\gets \\text{rows}(\\mathbf{P})$\n\\State $C_{\\text{expert}} \\gets \\lceil (T / E) \\cdot C_{\\text{factor}} \\rceil$\n\\State Initialize counts $\\mathbf{c} \\gets \\text{zeros of size } E, \\quad D \\gets 0$\n\\For{$t \\gets 0 \\textbf{ to } T - 1$}\n    \\State $e^* \\gets \\arg\\max_{0 \\le e < E} P_{t, e}$\n    \\If{$c[e^*] < C_{\\text{expert}}$}\n        \\State $c[e^*] \\gets c[e^*] + 1$\n    \\Else\n        \\State $D \\gets D + 1$ \\Comment{Buffer overflow: token dropped}\n    \\EndIf\n\\EndFor\n\\State $f_e \\gets [c[e] / T \\text{ for } e \\in \\{0, \\dots, E-1\\}]$\n\\State $P_e \\gets [\\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} P_{t, e} \\text{ for } e \\in \\{0, \\dots, E-1\\}]$\n\\State $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} \\gets E \\sum_{e=0}^{E-1} f_e \\cdot P_e$\n\\State \\Return $\\{\\text{aux\\_loss}: \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}}, \\text{expert\\_token\\_counts}: \\mathbf{c}, \\text{dropped\\_tokens\\_count}: D, \\text{token\\_drop\\_rate}: D / T\\}$\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\section{Theoretical Guarantees: Global Minimum under Uniform Routing}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Global Minimum of Load Balancing Loss]\n\\label{thm:moe_aux_min}\nLet $\\mathbf{f}, \\mathbf{P} \\in \\Delta^{E-1}$ lie on the probability simplex. The auxiliary loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = E \\sum_{e=1}^E f_e P_e$ achieves its global minimum of $1.0$ if and only if both routing probabilities and dispatched tokens are uniformly balanced:\n\\begin{equation}\nf_e = \\frac{1}{E} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad P_e = \\frac{1}{E} \\quad \\forall e \\in \\{1, \\dots, E\\} \\implies \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = 1.0\n\\end{equation}\n\\end{theorem}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nBy Cauchy-Schwarz inequality on $\\mathbb{R}^E$:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\sum_{e=1}^E f_e P_e \\ge \\frac{1}{E} \\left( \\sum_{e=1}^E f_e \\right) \\left( \\sum_{e=1}^E P_e \\right) = \\frac{1}{E} (1)(1) = \\frac{1}{E}\n\\end{equation}\nMultiplying by $E$:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = E \\sum_{e=1}^E f_e P_e \\ge E \\cdot \\frac{1}{E} = 1.0\n\\end{equation}\nEquality holds if and only if $f_e$ and $P_e$ are collinear constants, requiring $f_e = P_e = 1/E$. Under complete collapse where all tokens route to expert $1$, $f_1 = P_1 = 1$, giving maximum penalty $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = E \\cdot (1 \\cdot 1) = E$.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\section{Complexity Analysis}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Time Complexity:} $\\Theta(T \\cdot E)$ operations to evaluate probabilities and dispatch counts.\n    \\item \\textbf{Space Complexity:} $\\Theta(E)$ storage for counts and probability averages.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{Worked Numerical Example}\nLet $T = 2, E = 2, C_{\\text{factor}} = 1.0 \\implies C_{\\text{expert}} = \\lceil (2/2) \\cdot 1.0 \\rceil = 1$.\nRouter probabilities: $\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item Token 0 selects expert $0$ ($\\max=0.8$). $c[0] = 0 < 1 \\implies c[0] = 1$.\n    \\item Token 1 selects expert $0$ ($\\max=0.9$). $c[0] = 1 = C_{\\text{expert}} \\implies$ overflow! Dropped count $D = 1$.\n    Counts: $\\mathbf{c} = [1, 0]$.\n    \\item Fractions: $f_0 = 1/2 = 0.5, f_1 = 0/2 = 0.0$.\n    \\item Probability sums: $P_0 = (0.8 + 0.9)/2 = 0.85$, $P_1 = (0.2 + 0.1)/2 = 0.15$.\n    \\item Auxiliary loss:\n    $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{aux}} = 2 \\times (0.5 \\times 0.85 + 0.0 \\times 0.15) = 2 \\times 0.425 = 0.85$.\n    \\item Token drop rate: $1 / 2 = 0.5$ ($50\\%$).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{shazeer2017} N.~Shazeer et al., ``Outrageously large neural networks: The sparsely-gated mixture-of-experts layer,'' in \\emph{ICLR}, 2017.\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"
